```

1. A program that uses a vector and a loop to find an average:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
int main() {
    std::vector<int> scores = {90, 85, 77};
    int total = 0;
    for (int s : scores) {
        total += s;
    }
    std::cout << "Average: " << total / scores.size() << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```
The output is Average: 84. The total is 252, and the loop adds each score from the vector.

2. A class:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
class Student {
public:
    std::string name;
    int grade;
    Student(std::string n, int g) : name(n), grade(g) {}
    void show() { std::cout << name << ": " << grade << std::endl; }
};
```
Creating Student s("Ana", 92); and calling s.show(); prints Ana: 92.

3. A pointer and a smart pointer:
```
int x = 10;
int* p = &x;
*p = 20;
auto q = std::make_unique<int>(5);
```
The pointer p holds the address of x, and *p = 20 changes x to 20. The smart pointer q owns an integer with the value 5, and frees it automatically when q goes out of scope. Smart pointers need the memory header.

4. C++ compared with Java, the language of this course:
```
Feature            C++                          Java
Runs as            native machine code          bytecode on the JVM
Memory             manual, or smart pointers    garbage collector
Pointers           yes                          no explicit pointers
Multiple parents   allowed for classes          only through interfaces
Portability        compile again for each OS    same bytecode runs anywhere
Speed              usually faster               slightly slower start and more memory
```
